              "content": "How Quantities Combine: Understanding Data"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.853,
            0.071,
            0.88,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.111,
            0.315,
            0.131
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10.1.2 Mixtures"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.142,
            0.699,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us consider what happens when things are mixed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.167,
            0.884,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Two glasses of equal quantities of lemonade are prepared. One glass has 10% jaggery and the other has 20% jaggery. If we mix the lemonade from both glasses, what is the concentration of jaggery in the mixture?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.245,
            0.882,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since both the glasses contain equal amounts of lemonade, the mixture will have 15% jaggery. We can also arrive at this mathematically —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.288,
            0.713,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Suppose y is the quantity of lemonade in each glass."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.313,
            0.652,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The concentration of jaggery in the mixture is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.344,
            0.677,
            0.494
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &\\frac{quantity\\ ofjaggery\\ in\\ the\\ mixture}{quantity\\ ofthe mixture}\\\\ &= \\frac{\\frac{10}{100}y + \\frac{20}{100}y}{y + y}\\\\ &= \\frac{0.1y + 0.2y}{y + y} = \\frac{0.3y}{2y} = 0.15.\\\\ \\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.499,
            0.883,
            0.579
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since the glasses had equal quantities of lemonade, the concentration of jaggery in the combined mixture is midway between the concentrations of jaggery in the individual glasses. This is similar to the cycling example, where all weeks had equal numbers of days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.582,
            0.883,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A bowl of 500 mL lemonade has 10% jaggery. A glass of 200 mL lemonade has 20% jaggery. If we mix the lemonade from the bowl and the glass, what is the concentration of jaggery in the mixture?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.659,
            0.881,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you estimate what part of the mixture is jaggery? Is it 15%, or is it more or less? Why do you think so?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.697,
            0.882,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since the bowl has a larger quantity of lemonade, the concentration of jaggery in the mixture will be closer to 10% (less than midway)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.743,
            0.748,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The concentration of jaggery in the mixture is given by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.766,
            0.683,
            0.902
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_3_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &\\frac{quantity\\ ofjaggey\\ in\\ the\\ mixture}{quantity\\ ofthe mixture}\\\\ &\\quad = \\frac{500 \\times 0.1 + 200 \\times 0.2}{500 + 200}\\\\ &\\quad = \\frac{50 + 40}{700} = \\frac{90}{700} \\approx 0.13.\\\\ \\end{aligned}"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
